    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86362.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 66,
    "turn_text": "Absolutely, I was going to say that because, you know, I'm, you know, I don't hold back and the fact is that that we had There were a lot of issues with democracy in Armenia from 1991 to 2018. And it is a young, the democracy part is about three, four years old. And it could revert back to having oligarchs control parts of government. So it is really important. And I think Armenia is put in a really fragile position. where it doesn't have a lot of natural resources to offer. It doesn't have a lot of, you know, neighbors that are friendly and Russia is not very reliable. Thank goodness for, you know, the diaspora and people like you, Congressman, who advocate for human rights. I don't want to take too much of your time. I have just Well, my last question would be on a positive note going forward and being in the solution, right? So people, you know, some people are pessimistic. We're not hopeful. what tangible and you know just realistic hopeful things you have to say or see or of course you don't have a you know crystal ball but do you see coming up that we can sort of be hopeful about if you will.",
